# Example Econet configuration for b-em
#
# Originally based on the BeebEm Econet config by Rob O'Donnell
# (robert@irrelevant.com), updated for b-em.
#
# To use this file, copy it to ~/.config/b-em/econet.cfg (create the
# directory if it doesn't exist) and edit it for your setup.  Lines
# starting with # are comments.
#
# b-em looks for this file via find_cfg_file("econet", ".cfg"), which
# searches (in order) $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/b-em, $HOME/.config/b-em and the
# current directory.
#
#
# General options
# =-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
#
# AUNMODE - if set, b-em talks AUN (Acorn Universal Networking) over UDP,
# which is how it can communicate with real Econet hardware (via a bridge
# such as the PiEconetBridge) and with other emulators.  If set to 0, b-em
# falls back to a simple mode where each "station" below is just another
# local/networked b-em instance using b-em's own non-AUN wire format.
AUNMODE 1
#
# LEARN - if set, when b-em receives a packet from an IP address it
# doesn't recognise, it looks in AUNmap.cfg for an AddMap entry whose /24
# matches that IP to work out an Econet network number (station number is
# the last octet of the sender's IP), and adds/updates that station's
# entry in the table with the sender's IP/port. Useful so you don't have
# to list every station you might hear from.
LEARN 0
#
# AUNSTRICT - "STRICT" here means strict adherence to the AUN addressing
# convention (a host's station number equals the last octet of its IP
# address), not strict validation of destinations - if you're expecting
# this to make b-em *drop* packets to unknown destinations, it's the
# opposite: assuming the convention holds gives b-em a rule it can use to
# derive an address, so it guesses instead of dropping. With AUNSTRICT
# off there's no such rule, so an unlisted destination is simply unknown
# and the packet goes nowhere.
#
# If set, and the Beeb sends to a destination station/network
# that isn't in the table below, b-em will guess a destination IP/port to
# send to rather than dropping the packet: for destination network 0 (or
# b-em's own AUN network, per AUNmap.cfg), it guesses b-em's own /24 with
# the destination station number as the last octet; for any other
# destination network, it looks in AUNmap.cfg for an AddMap entry for that
# network and does the same with that entry's /24. Either way it uses the
# default AUN port 32768. Entries below always take priority over a guess.
AUNSTRICT 1
#
# MASSAGENETS - AUN network numbers are conventionally 128 and above, while
# "real" Econet network numbers seen by the emulated Beeb are below 128.
# If NOT set, b-em ORs 128 into the network number of every packet it
# sends, which it then matches against the network numbers in the table
# below when checking replies. If set, it sends the network number
# unchanged.
#
# For a typical setup using network 0 throughout (as in the examples
# below), you want this set to 1: with it unset, outgoing packets would
# be tagged network 128 but your table entries say network 0, and reply
# matching would fail. (There's no equivalent receive-side translation -
# that part of the option is currently unimplemented.)
MASSAGENETS 1
#
#
# Timing options
# =-=-=-=-=-=-=-
#
# These control internal timing of the emulated Econet four-way handshake
# and AUN translation. All four values below are in b-em's "EconetCycles"
# units: 1 EconetCycle = 128 CPU cycles, which is ~64us at a 2MHz emulated
# bus clock. (This differs from BeebEm, which uses raw CPU cycles for the
# same-named options - don't reuse BeebEm values here unscaled.)
#
# Getting these wrong tends to show up as "No reply", "Line Jammed",
# garbled *CAT output, or the emulation hanging mid-transfer.
#
# FLAGFILLTIMEOUT - how long b-em will flag-fill the line while waiting for
# a reply from the emulated/real fileserver during the four-way handshake.
# b-em's default is 256 (~16ms), which is fine for purely local/emulated
# fileservers.
#
# This value is also used (x10) as the timeout for a different case: when
# b-em is talking to a real fileserver via an AUN bridge (e.g. the
# PiEconetBridge), the bridge ACKs the request immediately over UDP but the
# real fileserver's reply can take much longer to arrive - we've measured
# over a second for some Pi-based fileserver operations such as "Get byte"
# and "Get multiple bytes" when they hit a disk sector boundary. If this
# value is too low you'll see "Econet: bridge response wait timed out for
# stn=... net=...".
#
# 50000 (~3.2s locally, ~32s for the x10 bridge-wait case) is a good
# starting point if you're bridging to real hardware over a Pi.
FLAGFILLTIMEOUT 256
#
# SCACKTIMEOUT - delay (AUN mode only) before b-em delivers a faked scout
# acknowledgement to the emulated Beeb. AUN's UDP exchange has no separate
# scout/ack step, so b-em fakes this part of the four-way handshake after
# a short delay. b-em's default of 4 (~256us) is normally fine.
SCACKTIMEOUT 4
#
# FOURWAYTIMEOUT - how long the four-way handshake as a whole can remain
# non-idle - i.e. the total time from the handshake starting until b-em
# gives up waiting for the other end (e.g. the fileserver's reply) and
# resets back to idle. The timer is set once when the handshake leaves
# idle and is not extended as it progresses through its stages.  b-em's
# default is 15625 (~1s).
#
# If you're bridging to a real fileserver that can occasionally take more
# than a second to reply (see FLAGFILLTIMEOUT above), this can fire just
# before the real reply arrives, leaving b-em and the emulated NFS ROM out
# of sync and the transfer hanging. If you're seeing hangs partway through
# a load when bridging to real hardware, try raising this to match
# FLAGFILLTIMEOUT, e.g. 50000 (~3.2s).
FOURWAYTIMEOUT 15625
#
# TIMEBETWEENBYTES - rate-limits how fast b-em drains bytes the Beeb
# writes to the ADLC's transmit FIFO (Beeb -> network direction only;
# incoming bytes use a separate fixed delay), simulating realistic
# on-wire timing so the NMI-driven transfer routine in the NFS/ANFS ROM
# behaves as it would talking to a real Econet. b-em's default of 128
# (~8ms/byte) is the value to leave this at - other values have proven
# unreliable in testing.
TIMEBETWEENBYTES 128
#
#
# List of reachable computers
# =-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
#
# Each line below maps an Econet network/station number to an IP
# address and UDP port:
#
#   <network> <station> <ip> <port>
#
# A line starting with a digit is parsed as a <network> <station> <ip>
# <port> entry (a missing or invalid field is reported as a warning in
# the log and the line is skipped). A line starting with anything other
# than '#' or a digit is instead treated as one of the option lines from
# the sections above, and an unrecognised option name is also reported as
# a warning - so make sure any comments you add start with '#'.
#
# - AUNSTRICT's guessing (described above) always assumes the default AUN
#   port 32768. If AUNSTRICT and LEARN are both on, and every host in
#   your setup uses port 32768 and follows the AUNmap.cfg subnet/station
#   convention, you may not need any entries here at all. Entries here
#   always override a guess, so add one whenever a host needs a
#   non-default port (as in the PiEconetBridge example below) or address.
# - If AUNSTRICT/LEARN are off, every station you want to talk to must be
#   listed here, and ideally every machine on the network should have a
#   matching list.
# - Network number 0 means "my local Econet segment" - this is what you
#   want for stations reached via AUN (other emulators, bridges exposing
#   single hosts, etc). Use a non-zero network number only for stations
#   that the bridge exposes as a distinct Econet network (e.g. a real
#   wired Econet segment on the far side of a bridge).
#
#
# Example: another b-em/BeebEm instance on the same PC, fileserver at
# station 254 and a couple of client stations, addressed via localhost
# with one UDP port per station:
# 0 254 127.0.0.1 32768
# 0 101 127.0.0.1 10101
# 0 102 127.0.0.1 10102
#
# Example: a real Econet fileserver exposed over AUN by the PiEconetBridge
# (https://github.com/cr12925/PiEconetBridge) running on a Raspberry Pi
# at 192.168.1.225. The bridge's own AUN map config decides the port for
# each station - here it's been set up to use 10000+<station>, but check
# your bridge's AUN map for the ports it actually uses:
# 0 254 192.168.1.225 10254
#
# Other emulators (e.g. BeebEm) on a different PC can join the same AUN
# network too, as long as they're registered in the Pi's AUN map and
# listed here with their own IP/port:
# 0 105 192.168.1.218 10105
#
# Example: a real, wired Econet segment attached to the Pi's Econet HAT,
# exposed by the bridge as Econet network 1 via
#   EXPOSE HOST 1.1 ON PORT *:10001
#   EXPOSE HOST 1.2 ON PORT *:10002
# Station 1.1 and 1.2 then appear to b-em as network 1, stations 1 and 2:
# 1 1 192.168.1.225 10001
# 1 2 192.168.1.225 10002
